module nfkbTranslocationDetection
module geneExpressionDetection
module cellViabilityDetection

manifest TNFalpha
manifest IL1beta
manifest LPS
manifest PAM2CSK4
manifest PBS
manifest CompleteMedia
manifest PluronicF127
manifest FluorescentNFkBCells
manifest FDA_PI_Stain

instructions:

// ============================================================
// Processing Stimulus Dynamics by the NF-kB Network in
// Single Cells.
// Son, Wang, Keisham, Tay - Experimental & Molecular Medicine
// (2023) 55:2531-2540.
// DOI: 10.1038/s12276-023-01133-7
//
// This BioScript encodes the core microfluidic single-cell
// NF-kB signaling protocol described in the review, covering:
//   (1) Device priming and single-cell loading
//   (2) Pulsatile TNF-a stimulation (low vs. high frequency)
//   (3) Sequential multi-ligand stimulation
//         (TNF-a -> IL-1b -> LPS -> PAM2CSK4)
//   (4) NF-kB translocation detection at single-cell resolution
//   (5) Downstream gene expression readout
//   (6) Cell viability QC gate
//
// BIOLOGICAL CONTEXT:
//   Cells used are mouse fibroblasts (or equivalent cell line)
//   stably expressing fluorescently tagged NF-kB (e.g. p65-RFP
//   or p65-GFP) enabling real-time nuclear translocation
//   tracking by live fluorescence microscopy. The microfluidic
//   device provides high-throughput, precise temporal control
//   over cytokine delivery to cells in isolated chambers
//   (up to 64 experimental conditions in parallel, Son et al.
//   Sci. Signal. 2021).
//
// STIMULATION STRATEGY:
//   The paper tests three classes of dynamic input:
//     (A) Pulsatile: repeated short TNF-a exposures at varying
//         inter-pulse intervals (60, 100, 200 min periods).
//     (B) Increasing dose: stepwise or ramp increases in TNF-a
//         or IL-1b, revealing differentiator-like NF-kB behaviour
//         (response tracks change in level, not absolute dose).
//     (C) Sequential multi-ligand: TNF-a followed by IL-1b,
//         LPS, and PAM2CSK4 in defined 8 h sequences, encoding
//         stimulus identity and order into NF-kB dynamics via
//         ligand-specific feedback memory (IRAK1, IkBd, A20).
//
// NOTE ON STIMULATION ABSTRACTION:
//   `mix` operations model microfluidic perfusion of stimulus
//   into a cell-containing channel chamber. They do not
//   represent DMF electrode mixing. Each `dispense` abstracts
//   a programmable microfluidic valve-controlled bolus.
//   Duration arguments to `mix` encode the perfusion window
//   (i.e. the pulse duration or the incubation period).
//
// NOTE ON TIME UNITS:
//   BioScript supports `s` (seconds) and `m` (minutes) only.
//   All durations are expressed in `m` or `s` accordingly.
//   Inter-pulse recovery periods (e.g. 200 min) are encoded
//   as `heat` steps at physiological temperature (37 C) to
//   model the incubation/wash period between pulses.
//
// CFG STRUCTURE:
//   BB1  main                -- entry: priming, cell loading,
//                               pulsatile stimulation arm,
//                               NF-kB translocation detection
//   BB2  nfkbPass            -- nfkbSignal > 0: oscillatory
//                               NF-kB response confirmed;
//                               proceed to sequential stimulation
//   BB3  nfkbFail            -- nfkbSignal <= 0: no translocation
//                               detected; drain cells
//   BB4  nfkbJoin            -- join: sequential ligand arm begins
//   BB5  seqPass             -- seqSignal > 0: sequence-encoded
//                               NF-kB dynamics confirmed;
//                               proceed to gene expression readout
//   BB6  seqFail             -- seqSignal <= 0: no sequence
//                               encoding detected; drain
//   BB7  seqJoin             -- join: gene expression & viability
//   BB8  expressionPass      -- expressionSignal > 0: target gene
//                               upregulation confirmed; dispose
//   BB9  expressionFail      -- expressionSignal <= 0: no
//                               expression; drain
//   BB10 finalCleanup        -- terminal: dispose all QC signals
// ============================================================

// ============================================================
// BB1: main
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1: Prime microfluidic device.
//   2% Pluronics F-127 in PBS perfused for 5 min to prevent
//   non-specific cell adhesion to PDMS channel walls.
//   Consistent with standard microfluidic cell culture setup
//   (Son et al. Biosens. Bioelectron. 2023; Samlali et al.
//   Small 2020).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

primingPBS      = dispense 1 units of PBS
primingPluronic = dispense 1 units of PluronicF127
primingMix      = mix primingPBS with primingPluronic for 5m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2: Load fluorescently tagged NF-kB reporter cells.
//   Cells expressing p65-RFP (or equivalent) are flowed into
//   individual microfluidic chambers at low density to achieve
//   single-cell isolation per chamber. This enables real-time
//   tracking of NF-kB nuclear translocation by fluorescence
//   microscopy (Nelson et al. J. Cell Sci. 2002; Tay et al.
//   Nature 2010).
//   NOTE: `mix` abstracts channel-based cell loading; no
//   reagent mixing in the DMF sense occurs at this step.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

reporterCells   = dispense 1 units of FluorescentNFkBCells
loadedCells     = mix reporterCells with primingMix for 2m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 3: Equilibration.
//   Allow cells to settle and adhere in chambers at 37 C,
//   5% CO2 for 30 min prior to stimulation. Ensures baseline
//   NF-kB is cytoplasmic before stimulus delivery.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

heat loadedCells at 37c for 30m

// ============================================================
// PULSATILE STIMULATION ARM (Pulse 1 of 3)
// Low-frequency pulsatile TNF-a (200 min inter-pulse period).
// From Ashall et al. Science 2009 and Kellogg & Tay Cell 2015.
// Low-frequency pulses (>200 min period) allow full NF-kB
// system reset between pulses, maintaining full-amplitude
// response across repeated stimulations.
//
// Protocol: 15 min TNF-a pulse -> 185 min wash/recovery ->
//           repeat x3. 15-min pulses at ~10 ng/mL TNF-a.
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 4: Pulsatile TNF-a - Pulse 1.
//   Deliver TNF-a (10 ng/mL) for 15 min via programmable
//   valve. Continuous TNF-a is required for oscillatory
//   NF-kB dynamics; brief pulses elicit single translocation
//   events (Nelson et al. Science 2004; Turner et al.
//   J. Cell Sci. 2010).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

tnfPulse1       = dispense 1 units of TNFalpha
stimulation1    = mix loadedCells with tnfPulse1 for 15m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 5: Inter-pulse wash and recovery (Pulse 1 -> Pulse 2).
//   Replace TNF-a with PBS to terminate stimulation.
//   185 min recovery at 37 C allows NF-kB to return to
//   cytoplasm, IKK to reset via A20/IkBa negative feedback,
//   and cells to exit the refractory state before next pulse.
//   Total inter-pulse period = 185 + 15 = 200 min.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

washPBS1        = dispense 1 units of PBS
washedCells1    = mix stimulation1 with washPBS1 for 5m
heat washedCells1 at 37c for 185m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 6: Pulsatile TNF-a - Pulse 2.
//   Second 15 min TNF-a pulse. At 200 min period, NF-kB
//   oscillation is fully reset; second pulse produces
//   full-amplitude response (Ashall et al. Science 2009).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

tnfPulse2       = dispense 1 units of TNFalpha
stimulation2    = mix washedCells1 with tnfPulse2 for 15m

washPBS2        = dispense 1 units of PBS
washedCells2    = mix stimulation2 with washPBS2 for 5m
heat washedCells2 at 37c for 185m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 7: Pulsatile TNF-a - Pulse 3.
//   Third 15 min TNF-a pulse. Confirms entrainment or
//   persistent NF-kB response amplitude at low-frequency
//   pulsatile regime. Kellogg & Tay (Cell 2015) showed noise
//   in NF-kB oscillation facilitates entrainment and
//   downstream gene expression over a range of frequencies.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

tnfPulse3       = dispense 1 units of TNFalpha
stimulation3    = mix washedCells2 with tnfPulse3 for 15m

washPBS3        = dispense 1 units of PBS
washedCells3    = mix stimulation3 with washPBS3 for 5m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 8: NF-kB translocation detection (pulsatile arm).
//   nfkbTranslocationDetection module abstracts live-cell
//   fluorescence imaging of p65-RFP nuclear-to-cytoplasmic
//   ratio (N:C ratio) measured in single cells at each time
//   point. Translocation confirmed by N:C ratio > 1 at peak.
//   Image acquisition: every 3-5 min over the full 8 h
//   stimulation window (consistent with Ashall et al. and
//   Kellogg & Tay protocols).
//   Detection window: 90 s per chamber scan.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

nfkbSignal      = detect nfkbTranslocationDetection on washedCells3 for 90s

// ============================================================
// BB2 / BB3: NF-kB translocation QC gate (pulsatile arm)
// Pass (BB2): nfkbSignal > 0 -> oscillatory NF-kB translocation
//             confirmed across all three pulses; proceed to
//             sequential ligand stimulation arm (BB4).
// Fail (BB3): nfkbSignal <= 0 -> no detectable translocation;
//             cells may be unhealthy or reporter not expressed;
//             drain and discard this chamber population.
// Both branches converge at BB4 (nfkbJoin).
// ============================================================

if (nfkbSignal > 0) {
    dispose washedCells3
} else {
    drain washedCells3
}

// ============================================================
// BB4: nfkbJoin
// Dispose pulsatile arm NF-kB signal. Control falls into
// the sequential multi-ligand stimulation arm (BB5/BB6).
// A fresh population of loaded cells is used for this arm
// (independent experimental condition on same device).
// ============================================================

dispose nfkbSignal

// ============================================================
// SEQUENTIAL MULTI-LIGAND STIMULATION ARM
// From Wang, Son et al. Cell Rep. 2022 (ref 15 in paper).
// Four-ligand sequence over 8 h: TNF-a -> IL-1b -> LPS -> PAM.
// Each ligand delivered for ~2 h (120 min) then washed off.
// NF-kB dynamics encode stimulus identity and sequence via
// ligand-specific negative feedback memory:
//   - IRAK1 autophosphorylation: encodes IL-1b / LPS memory
//     (cross-tolerance between IL-1b and LPS; not TNF-a)
//   - IkBd (p100 homodimer): broad cross-tolerance on 24 h scale
//   - A20: upstream of IKK; inhibits signaling from most ligands
// The sequence produces distinct NF-kB oscillation patterns
// for each ligand-order combination (Wang et al. Cell Rep. 2022).
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 9: Load fresh reporter cells for sequential arm.
//   New cell chamber or washed chamber re-loaded with
//   p65-RFP reporter cells. Same priming and equilibration
//   as Steps 1-3.
//   NOTE: primingMix2 / loadedCells2 are independent variables;
//   they do not reuse the pulsatile arm cell population.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

primingPBS2     = dispense 1 units of PBS
primingPluronic2 = dispense 1 units of PluronicF127
primingMix2     = mix primingPBS2 with primingPluronic2 for 5m

reporterCells2  = dispense 1 units of FluorescentNFkBCells
loadedCells2    = mix reporterCells2 with primingMix2 for 2m
heat loadedCells2 at 37c for 30m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 10: Sequential stimulus 1 - TNF-a (0-120 min).
//   TNF-a (10 ng/mL) delivered for 120 min.
//   TNF-a signals via TNFR -> TRADD/RIP1/TRAF2/5 -> TAK1 -> IKK.
//   Does NOT activate IRAK1/MyD88 myddosome pathway.
//   Primary NF-kB response: damped oscillation, 1-3 peaks.
//   A20 induced as negative feedback, partially suppressing
//   subsequent TRAF2/5-dependent signals.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

tnfSeq          = dispense 1 units of TNFalpha
seqStim1        = mix loadedCells2 with tnfSeq for 120m

washSeq1        = dispense 1 units of PBS
seqWashed1      = mix seqStim1 with washSeq1 for 5m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 11: Sequential stimulus 2 - IL-1b (120-240 min).
//   IL-1b (10 ng/mL) delivered for 120 min beginning at t=120m.
//   IL-1b signals via IL-1R -> MyD88/IRAK1/TRAF6 (myddosome).
//   After prior TNF-a, A20 levels are elevated. However,
//   IL-1b can overcome A20 inhibition (different receptor
//   complex) and produce NF-kB reactivation without attenuation
//   (Adamson et al. Nat. Commun. 2016; DeFelice et al.
//   Sci. Signal. 2019 - ref 64 in paper).
//   IRAK1 begins to autophosphorylate, encoding IL-1b memory.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

il1bSeq         = dispense 1 units of IL1beta
seqStim2        = mix seqWashed1 with il1bSeq for 120m

washSeq2        = dispense 1 units of PBS
seqWashed2      = mix seqStim2 with washSeq2 for 5m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 12: Sequential stimulus 3 - LPS (240-360 min).
//   LPS (100 ng/mL) delivered for 120 min beginning at t=240m.
//   LPS signals via TLR4 -> MyD88/IRAK1/TRAF6 (myddosome) AND
//   TRIF-TRAM pathway (after TLR4 endocytosis).
//   LPS and IL-1b share the IRAK1/MyD88 myddosome. If IRAK1 is
//   autophosphorylated and aggregated from prior IL-1b stimulation,
//   cells enter cross-tolerance state: LPS-driven NF-kB response
//   is attenuated (DeFelice et al. Sci. Signal. 2019 - ref 64).
//   Dose-dependent: 100 ng/mL LPS drives stronger IRAK1
//   clustering than lower doses, deepening cross-tolerance.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

lpsSeq          = dispense 1 units of LPS
seqStim3        = mix seqWashed2 with lpsSeq for 120m

washSeq3        = dispense 1 units of PBS
seqWashed3      = mix seqStim3 with washSeq3 for 5m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 13: Sequential stimulus 4 - PAM2CSK4 (360-480 min).
//   PAM2CSK4 (100 ng/mL) delivered for 120 min (t=360-480m).
//   PAM2CSK4 signals via TLR1/2 -> MyD88/IRAK1/TRAF6 (same
//   myddosome as IL-1b and LPS). After IRAK1 clustering from
//   prior IL-1b + LPS, PAM response is significantly attenuated.
//   Son et al. (Sci. Signal. 2021 - ref 13 in paper) showed
//   PAM exhibits a differentiator-like NF-kB response to
//   concentration CHANGES but NOT to absolute levels, unlike
//   TNF-a and IL-1b. Sequence memory in IRAK1 further modulates
//   PAM response amplitude in a dose-dependent manner.
//   Full 8 h sequence total: TNF(2h) -> IL-1b(2h) -> LPS(2h) ->
//   PAM(2h), encoding 4-ligand sequential identity as distinct
//   NF-kB oscillation patterns (Wang et al. Cell Rep. 2022).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

pamSeq          = dispense 1 units of PAM2CSK4
seqStim4        = mix seqWashed3 with pamSeq for 120m

washSeq4        = dispense 1 units of PBS
seqWashed4      = mix seqStim4 with washSeq4 for 5m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 14: NF-kB translocation detection (sequential arm).
//   Same nfkbTranslocationDetection module applied to the
//   sequential stimulation cell population. Readout captures
//   the composite NF-kB dynamics across all 4 ligands:
//   oscillation period, amplitude, damping coefficient, and
//   inter-peak intervals encode the stimulus sequence and
//   feedback memory state of each cell.
//   Detection window: 90 s per chamber (consistent with Step 8).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

seqSignal       = detect nfkbTranslocationDetection on seqWashed4 for 90s

// ============================================================
// BB5 / BB6: Sequential stimulation NF-kB QC gate
// Pass (BB5): seqSignal > 0 -> sequence-encoded NF-kB dynamics
//             detected; distinct oscillation patterns for each
//             ligand confirmed; proceed to gene expression arm.
// Fail (BB6): seqSignal <= 0 -> NF-kB not responding to any
//             ligand in sequence; drain this chamber population.
// Both branches converge at BB7 (seqJoin).
// ============================================================

if (seqSignal > 0) {
    dispose seqWashed4
} else {
    drain seqWashed4
}

// ============================================================
// BB7: seqJoin
// Dispose sequential arm NF-kB signal. Control falls into
// the downstream gene expression and viability QC arm
// (BB8/BB9).
// ============================================================

dispose seqSignal

// ============================================================
// DOWNSTREAM GENE EXPRESSION AND CELL VIABILITY ARM
// The paper demonstrates that distinct NF-kB oscillation
// dynamics (amplitude, duration, period) produce distinct
// transcriptional outputs. Lane et al. (Cells 2017 - ref 120)
// linked NF-kB dynamics to proinflammatory gene expression
// profiles in single cells. Cheng et al. (Science 2021 - ref
// 14) showed macrophage NF-kB oscillations produce lasting
// epigenetic changes. This arm validates gene expression
// downstream of the sequential stimulation.
//
// Cell viability is assessed with FDA/PI staining consistent
// with Samlali et al. (Small 2020) to confirm stimulation
// protocol does not induce significant apoptosis.
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 15: Downstream gene expression readout.
//   After 8 h multi-ligand sequential stimulation, cells are
//   lysed (or fixed for smFISH) to assess mRNA levels of
//   NF-kB target genes (e.g. IL-6, CXCL8/IL-8, IkBa, A20,
//   TNF-a). geneExpressionDetection abstracts quantitative
//   RT-PCR or single-molecule FISH readout, normalised to
//   housekeeping genes. NF-kB oscillation period and amplitude
//   in the preceding imaging window are correlated with
//   target gene expression levels at the single-cell level
//   (Lane et al. Cells 2017; Wang et al. Cell Rep. 2022).
//   Detection window: 30 min for lysis + qPCR abstraction.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

expressCells    = dispense 1 units of FluorescentNFkBCells
expressMedia    = dispense 1 units of CompleteMedia
expressMix      = mix expressCells with expressMedia for 5m

expressionSignal = detect geneExpressionDetection on expressMix for 30m

// ============================================================
// BB8 / BB9: Gene expression QC gate
// Pass (BB8): expressionSignal > 0 -> NF-kB target gene
//             upregulation confirmed (IL-6, CXCL8, IkBa
//             elevated above baseline); dispose cells.
// Fail (BB9): expressionSignal <= 0 -> no detectable gene
//             expression response; cells may be refractory
//             or dead; drain.
// Both branches converge at BB10 (finalCleanup).
// ============================================================

if (expressionSignal > 0) {
    // --------------------------------------------------------
    // Cell viability QC (pass path only):
    //   FDA (live, green) and PI (dead, red) staining to
    //   confirm >80% viability across the 8 h stimulation.
    //   Viability is critical for interpreting NF-kB dynamics
    //   as true signaling responses, not death signals.
    //   (Son et al. Sci. Signal. 2021 - ref 13)
    //   Incubate stain for 10 min at room temperature.
    // --------------------------------------------------------
    fdaPiStain      = dispense 1 units of FDA_PI_Stain
    stainedCells    = mix expressMix with fdaPiStain for 10m

    viabilitySignal = detect cellViabilityDetection on stainedCells for 30s

    dispose stainedCells
    dispose viabilitySignal
    dispose expressMix
} else {
    drain expressMix
}

// ============================================================
// BB10: finalCleanup (terminal)
// Dispose all remaining QC signal droplets.
// Experiment ends. NF-kB stimulus dynamics protocol complete.
// ============================================================

dispose expressionSignal

// [terminal -- no further instructions; exit node for CFG]
